  '**For that figure to show that, three things would have to be true.** **One: that the applications counted are all the applications received** — the base. **Two: that _decided_ means what the complainants mean by it** — the definition. **Three: that the date recorded as the decision date is the date the applicant was told** — the measuring.',
  '**The third is the one I am asking about, and I am asking because of a date in your own return.** ⚠ **The improvement is dated from 1 September and the registry moved to the new file system on 1 September.** **I am not saying the figure is false. I am saying it assumes something your own return says changed in the same month.**',
  '**Why one and not three.** If the base or the definition is wrong, the claim survives in a reduced form. ⚠ **If the third fails, it goes entirely**, because a decision date that is not the date of telling is not a measure of delay at all.',
  '**And the one piece of evidence that would settle it.** **The gap in days between the recorded decision date and the date of posting, for the last two hundred applications.** If the gap is under three days, **the claim stands and I shall say so in writing.**',
